                %a{:href => "#references"} References
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            %a{:href => "#Freyd99"} Freyd (1999)
            gave a sharp and clever description of the commonalities and differences between
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+categories"} abelian categories
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/toposes"} toposes
            (or, in the first place, between abelian categories and
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pretoposes"} pretoposes
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/product"} product
            of a pretopos part and an abelian part.
          %p
            Here, the properties held in common between abelian categories and pretoposes are all exactness conditions; in the AT set-up, the decisive difference between abelian categories and pretoposes is concentrated solely in the behavior of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/initial+object"} initial object
            with respect to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+product"} cartesian product
            (being in one case the cartesian monoidal unit, i.e. an element that is universally absorbed into any other, and in the other an absorbing element, i.e. an element that absorbs any other element into itself, aka a zero element or annihilating element).
          %p There is no research we know of on AT categories. One might say that they were invented by Freyd really for the sole theoretical purpose of demonstrating the commonalities and differences between abelian categories and pretopos categories, not because AT categories are found in nature. Indeed, a “typical” AT category is something of a mythological creature with the head of an abelian lion and the body of a pretopos dragon, and serves no purpose as such, even though pure lions and pure dragons themselves serve a purpose. Some of the axioms, particular axiom “AE” below, seem expressly designed to fuse them together in ad hoc fashion.
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p Freyd introduces some baseline assumptions on the categories in question:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finitely+complete+category"}> Finite completeness
                ,
            %li
              %p
                Presence of an
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/initial+object"} initial object
            %li
              %p
                Presence of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pushout"} pushouts
                for pairs of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphism"} arrows
                one of which is
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monomorphism"} monic
            %li
              %p
                Presence of pushouts for
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/kernel+pair"} kernel pair
          %p
            Each of these assumptions obviously holds in any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+category"} abelian category
            and in any
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pretopos"} pretopos
          .num_remark
            %h6#remarks Remarks
            %p
              If we introduce predicates to express these four baseline assumptions, such as a predicate formula
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi p
                    %mo ,
                    %mi q
                    %mo ;
                    %mi f
                    %mo ,
                    %mi g
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P(p, q; f, g)
              whose meaning is “
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi p
                    %mo ,
                    %mi q
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (p, q)
              is a pushout pair for pairs of arrows
              = succeed "\u201D," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi f
                      %mo ,
                      %mi g
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (f, g)
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Horn+theory"} Horn clauses
              in the language generated by the baseline predicates. The first 8 axioms are in fact universal Horn clauses in these predicates.
            %p
              This might look like a side comment for logicians, but it might also signal that Freyd intends to invoke, if pressed for details of proofs, one of a battery of representation theorems, such as the representation theorem for regular categories whose precise formulation is in terms of Horn theories – see
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Categories%2C+Allegories"} Categories, Allegories
              for examples.
            %p
              Furthermore, by choosing a
              %em specific
              initial object, a specific pullback for each pair of arrows with common target, etc., an AT category can be cast as a model for a certain
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/essentially+algebraic+theory"} essentially algebraic theory
          %p The crucial difference between “abelianness” and “toposness” is concentrated in the following definition:
          %p
          #TypeATObjects.num_defn
            %h6 Definition
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
              denote the
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/initial+object"} initial object
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/object"} object
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              let
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi π
                          %mn 1
                        %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                        %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                        %mn 0
                        %mo ×
                        %mi X
                        %mo ⟶
                        %mn 0
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_1 \,\colon\, 0 \times X \longrightarrow 0
                  ,
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi π
                          %mn 2
                        %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                        %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                        %mn 0
                        %mo ×
                        %mi X
                        %mo ⟶
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_2 \,\colon\, 0 \times X \longrightarrow X
            %p
              denote the two product
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projections"} projections
            %p
              We say
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              is
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  of
                  %strong type T
                  if
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi π
                          %mn 1
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_1
                  is an isomorphism,
              %li
                %p
                  of
                  %strong type A
                  if
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi π
                          %mn 2
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_2
                  is an isomorphism.
            %p
          %p With this:
          %ul
            %li
              %p A pretopos will turn out to be precisely an AT category in which every object is of type T.
            %li
              %p An abelian category will turn out to be an AT category where every object is of type A.
          %p Here then are the AT exactness axioms. Again, each of them is satisfied in every abelian category and in every pretopos, and according to Freyd’s thesis, any exactness condition satisfied in both classes of categories is a logical consequence of this set of axioms. Some of Freyd’s remarks in his original posting are included in parentheses.
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              A category meeting the baseline assumptions above is an
              %strong AT category
              if the following 8 axioms plus axiom “AE” are satisfied.
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  The category is an effective
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/regular+category"} regular category
                %p (“Yes this can be stated as universal Horn conditions on pullbacks and the special pushouts mentioned above.”)
              %li
                %p
                  The arrow
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mn 0
                        %mo →
                        %mn 1
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 \to 1
                  is monic. (“Note that it follows that all maps from
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mn 0
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
                  are monic.”)
              %li
                %p
                  If
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                        %mi A
                        %mo →
                        %mi C
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i\colon A \to C
                  is
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monomorphism"} mono
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pushout"} pushout
                  square
                .maruku-equation
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mrow
                          %mtable
                            %mtr
                              %mtd
                                %mi A
                              %mtd
                                %mover
                                  %mo →
                                  %mi i
                              %mtd
                                %mi C
                            %mtr
                              %mtd
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                              %mtd
                              %mtd
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mtr
                              %mtd
                                %mi B
                              %mtd
                                %munder
                                  %mo →
                                  %mi j
                              %mtd
                                %mi D
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                        \\array{
                        A &amp; \stackrel{i}{\to} &amp; C \\
                        \\downarrow &amp; &amp; \downarrow \\
                        B &amp; \underset{j}{\to} &amp; D
                        }
                %p
                  is also a
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullback"} pullback
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi j
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} j
                  is mono.
              %li
                %p
                  The functor
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mn 0
                        %mo ×
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 \times -
                  preserves pushouts of kernel pairs, and pushouts of pairs of arrows one of which is monic.
              %li
                %p
                  If
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi f
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                        %mi B
                        %mo →
                        %mn 0
                        %mo ×
                        %mi C
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f\colon B \to 0 \times C
                  is
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/epimorphism"} epi
                  and
                .maruku-equation
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mrow
                          %mtable
                            %mtr
                              %mtd
                                %mi A
                              %mtd
                                %mo →
                              %mtd
                                %mi B
                            %mtr
                              %mtd
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                              %mtd
                              %mtd
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                                %mi f
                            %mtr
                              %mtd
                                %mn 0
                              %mtd
                                %mo →
                              %mtd
                                %mn 0
                                %mo ×
                                %mi C
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                        \\array{
                        A &amp; \to &amp; B \\
                        \\downarrow &amp; &amp; \downarrow f \\
                        0 &amp; \to &amp; 0 \times C
                        }
                %p
                  is a
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullback"} pullback
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pushout"} pushout
              %li
                %p
                  In the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/full+subcategory"} full subcategory
                  of type T objects (Def.
                  = succeed ")," do
                    %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#TypeATObjects"}
              %li
                %p
                  Define a functor
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi T
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
                  by the pushout diagram
                .maruku-equation
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mrow
                          %mtable
                            %mtr
                              %mtd
                                %mn 0
                                %mo ×
                                %mi X
                              %mtd
                                %mover
                                  %mo →
                                  %mrow
                                    %msub
                                      %mi π
                                      %mn 1
                              %mtd
                                %mn 0
                            %mtr
                              %mtd
                                %msub
                                  %mi π
                                  %mn 2
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                              %mtd
                              %mtd
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mtr
                              %mtd
                                %mi X
                              %mtd
                                %mo →
                              %mtd
                                %mi T
                                %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                        \\array{
                        0 \times X &amp; \stackrel{\pi_1}{\to} &amp; 0 \\
                        \\pi_2 \downarrow &amp; &amp; \downarrow \\
                        X &amp; \to &amp; T X
                        }
                %p
                  Then
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi T
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
                  preserves pullbacks.
              %li
                %p
                  Given a morphism
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi f
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                          %mi X
                          %mo →
                          %mi Y
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f\colon X \to Y
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi T
                        %mi f
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T f
                  and
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mn 0
                        %mo ×
                        %mi f
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 \times f
                  are isomorphisms, then so is
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi f
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
          %p
            This is the basic list of exactness assumptions which permit a sharp comparison between pretoposes and abelian categories. From this list alone one can prove that every AT category embeds faithfully in a product of a pretopos and an abelian category. To show that every AT category is
            %em equivalent
            to such a product, Freyd appends the following axiom involving an existential Horn condition:
          #AxiomAE.num_defn
            %h6#definition_3 Definition
            %p
              %strong axiom (AE)
            %p
              For every object
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ξ
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi T
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \xi\colon T X \to X
              such that
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ×
                    %mi X
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi π
                          %mn 1
                    %mi X
                    %mover
                      %mo ←
                      %mi ξ
                    %mi T
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 \times X \stackrel{\pi_1}{\to} X \stackrel{\xi}{\leftarrow} T X
            %p
              is a coproduct diagram, and the canonical map
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi T
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \to T X
              is a retraction of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ξ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \xi
          %p To bring actual toposes into the picture, Freyd adds some more axioms, but let’s first take stock and see what this gives us so far.
          %h2#basic_consequences_of_the_at_axioms Basic consequences of the AT axioms
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition Proposition
            %p
              For any
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              the unique map
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 \to X
              is monic.
          .proof
            %h6#proof Proof
            %p
              By axiom 2, the map
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                    %mo →
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 \to 1
              is monic. The pullback-preserving functor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo ×
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} - \times X
              preserves monos, so
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ×
                    %mi X
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mrow
                        %mo !
                        %mo ×
                        %msub
                          %mn 1
                          %mi X
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ×
                    %mi X
                    %mo ≅
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 \times X \stackrel{! \times 1_X}{\to} 1 \times X \cong X
            %p
              (which is just the second projection
              = succeed ")" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi π
                        %mn 2
                      %mo :
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ×
                      %mi X
                      %mo →
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_2: 0 \times X \to X
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                    %mo →
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ×
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 \to 0 \times X
              is monic since it has a retraction
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi π
                        %mn 1
                      %mo :
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ×
                      %mi X
                      %mo →
                      %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_1: 0 \times X \to0
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition_2 Proposition
            %p Binary coproducts exist.
          .proof
            %h6#proof_2 Proof
            %p
              Because we can take the pushout of a pair of monos
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 0
                      %mo →
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 \to X
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 0
                      %mo →
                      %mi Y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 \to Y
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition_3 Proposition
            %p Coproducts are disjoint.
          .proof
            %h6#proof_3 Proof
            %p
              By axiom 3, the two coprojections
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi i
                        %mi X
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi X
                      %mo →
                      %mi X
                      %mo +
                      %mi Y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i_X \colon X \to X + Y
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi i
                      %mi Y
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi Y
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                    %mo +
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i_Y \colon Y \to X + Y
              are monic, and their pullback is initial.
          %h2#category_of_type_a_objects_is_abelian Category of type A objects is abelian
          %p In this section we show that the full subcategory of type A objects forms an abelian category.
          #tozero.num_lemma
            %h6#lemma Lemma
            %p
              An object is of type A if and only if there exists a map to
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
          .proof
            %h6#proof_4 Proof
            %p
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              is of type A, then we clearly have
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %mo ≅
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ×
                      %mi X
                      %mover
                        %mo →
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi π
                            %mn 1
                      %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \cong 0 \times X \stackrel{\pi_1}{\to} 0
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi p
                      %mo :
                      %mi X
                      %mo →
                      %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p: X \to 0
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                    %mo →
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 \to 1
              is monic, there is at most one map
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                    %mo →
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y \to 0
              for any object
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y \to X
              are in natural bijective correspondence with maps
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                    %mo ×
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y \to X \times 0
              by composition with
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi π
                        %mn 1
                      %mo :
                      %mi X
                      %mo ×
                      %mn 0
                      %mo →
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_1: X \times 0 \to X
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_1
              is an isomorphism by the Yoneda lemma.
          #coreflective.num_prop
            %h6#proposition_4 Proposition
            %p The full subcategory of type A objects is coreflective.
          .proof
            %h6#proof_5 Proof
            %p
              By Lemma
              = succeed "," do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#tozero"}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              is equivalent to the subcategory
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ↪
                      %mi C
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mn 1
                      %mo ≃
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C/0 \hookrightarrow C/1 \simeq C
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ×
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A(X) = 0 \times X
          .num_lemma
            %h6#lemma_2 Lemma
            %p Objects of type A are closed under binary products, finite coproducts, subobjects, and quotient objects (= cokernels of kernel pairs).
          .proof
            %h6#proof_6 Proof
            %p
              This follows from the fact that the category of type A objects is the slice category
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C/0
          %p
            Before we prove the next lemma, recall that in a category with zero objects, a
            %strong kernel
            of an arrow
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f: A \to B
            is an equalizer of the pair
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 0
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mo →
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f, 0: A \stackrel{\to}{\to} B
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi K
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                        %mtd
                          %mi A
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                          %mi f
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mn 0
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                        %mtd
                          %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  K &amp; \to &amp; A \\
                  \\downarrow &amp; &amp; \downarrow f \\
                  0 &amp; \to &amp; B
                  }
          %p Cokernels are defined dually, and can be formulated dually as certain pushouts. Since pushouts along monos exist, we can take the cokernel of any mono, and in particular the cokernel of any kernel.
          .num_lemma
            %h6#lemma_3 Lemma
            %p In the category of type A objects, every mono is the kernel of its cokernel, and every epi is the cokernel of its kernel.
          .proof
            %h6#proof_7 Proof
            %p
              Of course,
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
              is a zero object in the category of type A objects. If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i\colon A \to B
              is a mono in the category of type A objects, then the pushout
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi A
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo →
                              %mi i
                          %mtd
                            %mi B
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mn 0
                          %mtd
                            %mo →
                          %mtd
                            %mi coker
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi i
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    A &amp; \stackrel{i}{\to} &amp; B \\
                    \\downarrow &amp; &amp; \downarrow \\
                    0 &amp; \to &amp; coker(i)
                    }
            %p
              exists and is also a pullback, by axiom 3, and hence
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
              is the kernel of its cokernel. Now suppose
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f: A \to C
              is an epi in the category of type A objects. Since
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ×
                      %mi C
                      %mo ≅
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 \times C \cong C
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi A
                      %mo →
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ×
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f\colon A \to 0 \times C
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi ker
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi f
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                            %mo →
                          %mtd
                            %mi A
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mi f
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mn 0
                          %mtd
                            %mo →
                          %mtd
                            %mn 0
                            %mo ×
                            %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    ker(f) &amp; \to &amp; A \\
                    \\downarrow &amp; &amp; \downarrow f \\
                    0 &amp; \to &amp; 0 \times C
                    }
            %p
              is also a pushout, so
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              is the cokernel of its kernel.
          .num_theorem
            %h6#theorem Theorem
            %p The category of type A objects is an abelian category.
          .proof
            %h6#proof_8 Proof
            %p
              Any category with zero objects, binary products and coproducts, and in which every mono is the kernel of its cokernel and every epi is the cokernel of its kernel, is in fact an abelian category. See Freyd-Scedrov,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Categories, Allegories
          %h2#category_of_type_t_objects_is_a_pretopos Category of type T objects is a pretopos
          %p
            Now we show that the full subcategory of type T objects is a pretopos. It is clear that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ×
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ≅
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 \times 0 \cong 0
            (since
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 0
                  %mo →
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 \to 1
            is monic), so
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
            is a type T object.
          #exactlyone.num_lemma
            %h6#lemma_4 Lemma
            %p
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              is type A and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
              is type T, then there exists exactly one map
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo →
                      %mi T
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \to T
          .proof
            %h6#proof_9 Proof
            %p
              There is exactly one morphism
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo →
                      %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \to 0
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \to T
              are in bijection with maps
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo →
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ×
                      %mi T
                      %mo ≅
                      %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \to 0 \times T \cong 0
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              has the property that there is exactly one map
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \to X
              for each type A object. Such objects
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              are closed under products, and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
              is such an object; therefore
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ×
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 \times X
              is such an object. On the other hand,
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ×
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 \times X
              is of type A since it projects to
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ×
                      %mi X
                      %mo →
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ×
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 \times X \to 0 \times X
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ×
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mn 0
                    %mo →
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ×
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 \times X \to 0 \to 0 \times X
              is the identity, so that
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ×
                      %mi X
                      %mo ≅
                      %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 \times X \cong 0
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              is of type T.
          #strict.num_cor
            %h6#corollary Corollary
            %p
              The initial object
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
              is
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+initial+object"} strict
              in the category of type T objects.
          .proof
            %h6#proof_10 Proof
            %p
              Given
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
              of type T and
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi T
                      %mo →
                      %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T \to 0
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
              is type A, and therefore by Lemma
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#exactlyone"}
              there is exactly one map
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi T
                      %mo →
                      %mi T
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T \to T
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                    %mo →
                    %mn 0
                    %mo →
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T \to 0 \to T
              is the identity, and of course so is
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 0
                      %mo →
                      %mi T
                      %mo →
                      %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 \to T \to 0
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
              is initial.
          #closed.num_cor
            %h6#corollary_2 Corollary
            %p The full subcategory of objects of type T is closed under products, coproducts, subobjects, and quotient objects.
          .proof
            %h6#proof_11 Proof
            %p
              Closure under products and subobjects is immediate from Lemma
              = succeed "." do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#exactlyone"}
          .num_theorem
            %h6#theorem_2 Theorem
            %p The full subcategory of objects of type T is a pretopos.
          .proof
            %h6#proof_12 Proof
            %p
              Corollary
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#closed"}
              gives finite completeness, coproducts, and quotients of kernel pairs. Axiom 6 gives that in the full subcategory of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
              objects, pushouts along monos are stable under pullback, and the initial object is stable under pullback in the category of T objects, because it is strict by Corollary
              = succeed "." do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#strict"}
          %h2#splitting_into_type_a_and_type_t_objects Splitting into type A and type T objects
          %p
            In this section we show that every AT category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            is the product of the abelian category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_A
            of type A objects and the pretopos
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_T
            of type T objects.
          %p
            Let us first observe that the coreflector (see Proposition
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#coreflective"}
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ×
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi C
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 \times {-}\colon C \to C_A
          %p is left exact (as all coreflectors are), and
          %ul
            %li
              %p Preserves the initial object (by axiom 2),
            %li
              %p Preserves pushouts of kernel pairs, and pushouts of pairs of arrows one of which is monic (axiom 4),
            %li
              %p And therefore also preserves coproducts.
          %p
            A functor between AT categories which is left exact and which preserves such classes of finite colimits may be called a
            = succeed "." do
              %strong morphism of AT categories
          %p
            Next let us observe that the subcategory
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mi T
                  %mo ↪
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_T \hookrightarrow C
            is
            = succeed ";" do
              %em reflective
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            defined above by means of a suitable pushout. The reflector clearly preserves any colimits that exist, and preserves pullbacks (by axiom 7), so
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi C
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T\colon C \to C_T
          %p is also a morphism of AT categories. Therefore we have a morphism of AT categories
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ×
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo ,
                  %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi C
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mi A
                  %mo ×
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F = \langle 0 \times {-}, T \rangle\colon C \to C_A \times C_T
          %p and now we wish to prove, under a suitable additional axiom (also satisfied by every abelian category and every pretopos), that this is an equivalence.
          .num_lemma
            %h6#lemma_5 Lemma
            %p
              The functor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
              is faithful.
          .proof
            %h6#proof_13 Proof
            %p
              The functor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
              is left exact and therefore preserves kernels. By axiom 8,
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
              reflects isomorphisms. It follows immediately from these two facts that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
              is faithful.
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark Remark
            %p With this result, Freyd’s “first task” is complete: any AT category may be faithfully represented in a product of a pretopos and an abelian category.
          %p
            At this point we bring in axiom
            = succeed "," do
              %strong AE
            = succeed "," do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#AxiomAE"}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T X
            is by definition the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cokernel"} cokernel
            appearing in the exact sequence
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ×
                  %mi X
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi π
                        %mn 2
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi coker
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 \times X \stackrel{\pi_2}{\to} X \to coker(\pi_2)
          %p
            and that axiom AE asserts that this exact sequence splits (with splitting
            = succeed ")," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ξ
                    %mo :
                    %mi coker
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \xi: coker(\pi_2) \to X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is the coproduct of the end terms.
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition_5 Proposition
            %p
              The functor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
              is full.
          .proof
            %p
              Suppose given maps
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo :
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ×
                      %mi X
                      %mo →
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ×
                      %mi Y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f: 0 \times X \to 0 \times Y
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi g
                      %mo :
                      %mi T
                      %mi X
                      %mo →
                      %mi T
                      %mi Y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g: T X \to T Y
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mn 0
                            %mo ×
                            %mi X
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo →
                              %mrow
                                %msub
                                  %mi π
                                  %mn 2
                          %mtd
                            %mi X
                          %mtd
                            %mo →
                          %mtd
                            %mi T
                            %mi X
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi f
                            %mo{:maxsize => "1.8em", :minsize => "1.8em"} ↓
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:maxsize => "1.8em", :minsize => "1.8em"} ↓
                            %mi g
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mn 0
                            %mo ×
                            %mi Y
                          %mtd
                            %munder
                              %mo →
                              %mrow
                                %msub
                                  %mi π
                                  %mn 2
                          %mtd
                            %mi Y
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo →
                              %mover
                                %mo ←
                                %mi ξ
                          %mtd
                            %mi T
                            %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    0 \times X &amp; \stackrel{\pi_2}{\to} &amp; X &amp; \to &amp; T X \\
                    f \Big\downarrow &amp; &amp; &amp; &amp; \Big\downarrow g \\
                    0 \times Y &amp; \underset{\pi_2}{\to} &amp; Y &amp; \stackrel{\overset{\xi}{\leftarrow}}{\to} &amp; T Y
                    }
            %p
              Since
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %mo =
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ×
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo +
                      %mi T
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X = (0 \times X) + T X
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ×
                      %mi X
                      %mo →
                      %mi Y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 \times X \to Y
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T X \to Y
              combine to give an arrow
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi f
                      %mo ,
                      %mi g
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo :
                      %mi X
                      %mo →
                      %mi Y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (f, g): X \to Y
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ×
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi f
                    %mo ,
                    %mi g
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 \times (f, g) = f
              (because the functor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ×
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 \times -
              kills the type T summands) and that
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi T
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi f
                      %mo ,
                      %mi g
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mi g
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T(f, g) = g
          .num_theorem
            %h6#theorem_3 Theorem
            %p
              The functor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
              is an equivalence.
          .proof
            %h6#proof_14 Proof
            %p
              All that is left to check is that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
              is essentially surjective, but this is clear because given a type A object
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              and a type T object
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ×
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo +
                    %mi Y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≅
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 \times (X + Y) \cong X
              (use axiom 4 plus the fact that
              = succeed ")," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ×
                      %mi Y
                      %mo ≅
                      %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 \times Y \cong 0
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo +
                    %mi Y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≅
                    %mi T
                    %mi X
                    %mo +
                    %mi T
                    %mi Y
                    %mo ≅
                    %mn 0
                    %mo +
                    %mi T
                    %mi Y
                    %mo ≅
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T(X + Y) \cong T X + T Y \cong 0 + T Y \cong Y
            %p which completes the proof.
          %p This completes Freyd’s “second task”.
          %h2#topostheoretic_considerations Topos-theoretic considerations
          %p
            In order to beef up the type T objects to a topos, Freyd imposes some extra structure on top of the AT axioms. Since it is now the topos axioms that are in the ascendant, I will christen these categories
            = succeed "." do
              %em TA categories
          %p
            Thus, a
            %strong TA category
            is an AT category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            together with
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Functions
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi P
                        %mo ,
                        %mi E
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                        %mi Ob
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi C
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo →
                        %mi Ob
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi C
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P, E\colon Ob(C) \to Ob(C)
            %li
              %p
                Functions
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi l
                        %mo ,
                        %mi r
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                        %mi Ob
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi C
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo →
                        %mi Mor
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi C
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} l, r\colon Ob(C) \to Mor(C)
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi l
                        %mi X
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                        %mi E
                        %mi X
                        %mo →
                        %mi P
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} l X\colon E X \to P X
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi r
                        %mi X
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                        %mi E
                        %mi X
                        %mo →
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r X\colon E X \to X
            %li
              %p
                An operation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Λ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Lambda
                which assigns to each pair of morphisms
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi f
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                        %mi R
                        %mo →
                        %mi Y
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f\colon R \to Y
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi g
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi R
                      %mo →
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g\colon R \to X
                (where
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi R
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
                are assumed to be type T), a morphism
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Λ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi f
                        %mo ,
                        %mi g
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                        %mi Y
                        %mo →
                        %mi P
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Lambda(f, g)\colon Y \to P X
          %p This data is to satisfy the following axioms:
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                      %mi l
                      %mi X
                      %mo ,
                      %mi r
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                      %mo :
                      %mi E
                      %mi X
                      %mo →
                      %mi P
                      %mi X
                      %mo ×
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle l X, r X \rangle: E X \to P X \times X
                is monic and
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi P
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P X
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi E
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E X
                are of type T,
            %li
              %p The composite of relations
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Y
                      %mover
                        %mo →
                        %mrow
                          %mi Λ
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi f
                          %mo ,
                          %mi g
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi P
                      %mi X
                      %mover
                        %mo →
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi l
                          %mi X
                          %msup
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mi op
                      %mi E
                      %mi X
                      %mover
                        %mo →
                        %mrow
                          %mi r
                          %mi X
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y \stackrel{\Lambda(f, g)}{\to} P X \stackrel{(l X)^{op}}{\to} E X \stackrel{r X}{\to} X
              %p is equal to
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Y
                      %mover
                        %mo →
                        %mrow
                          %msup
                            %mi f
                            %mi op
                      %mi R
                      %mover
                        %mo →
                        %mi g
                      %mi X
                      %mo ,
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y \stackrel{f^{op}}{\to} R \stackrel{g}{\to} X,
            %li
              %p If
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mrow
                        %mtable
                          %mtr
                            %mtd
                              %mi R
                            %mtd
                              %mover
                                %mo →
                                %mi g
                            %mtd
                              %mi E
                              %mi X
                          %mtr
                            %mtd
                              %mi f
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mtd
                            %mtd
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                              %mi l
                              %mi X
                          %mtr
                            %mtd
                              %mi Y
                            %mtd
                              %munder
                                %mo →
                                %mi h
                            %mtd
                              %mi T
                              %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      \\array{
                      R &amp; \stackrel{g}{\to} &amp; E X \\
                      f \downarrow &amp; &amp; \downarrow l X \\
                      Y &amp; \underset{h}{\to} &amp; T X
                      }
              %p
                is a pullback and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi R
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
                is of type T, then
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Λ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi f
                        %mo ,
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi r
                        %mi X
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ∘
                        %mi g
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo =
                        %mi h
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Lambda(f, (r X) \circ g) = h
          %p
            Freyd: “Note that in the full category of type-T objects,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            yields power-objects with
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi l
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} l
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi r
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r
            naming the universal relations. (The third axiom provides the uniqueness condition.)”
          %p
            And: “In any abelian category the only type T object is the zero object, which forces
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mo =
                  %mi E
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P = E = 0
            for abelian categories.”
          %p
            And finally: “It’s routine that both
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ×
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 \times {-}
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            preserve the new structure. We can now remove the existential from AE. Define
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ξ
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi T
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \xi\colon T X \to X
            as the image of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi r
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r X
          %ol
            %li
              For every
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ×
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                    %mover
                      %mo ←
                      %mi ξ
                    %mi T
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 \times X \to X \stackrel{\xi}{\leftarrow} T X
              is a coproduct diagram.“
          %p
            %em This section is likely to be rewritten and cleaned up.
          %h2#references References
          %ul
            %li#Freyd99
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+Freyd"} Peter Freyd
              in reply to
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Vaughan+Pratt"} Vaughan Pratt
              = succeed "," do
                %em Abelian-topos (AT) categories
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "http://www.mta.ca/~cat-dist/catlist/1999/atcat"} catlist:1999/atcat
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/Freyd_AT-categories.txt", :title => "txt"} txt
        .revisedby
